From c56895ae4bf7d67c192b1caeea72c1a04c90c867 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Avery Felts <averyfelts@aol.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 20:54:34 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 206/206] ntdll, server: msync is WineForge's USync 6

msync was WineForge's WFUSync v5, renamed. WineForge has replaced that with USync 6, and the
x86_64 runtime takes it with Wine 11.22: this brings the same msync to the arm64 runtime.

USync 6 keeps events, semaphores and mutexes in process where it can: pulse, set and reset of an
event, mutex release waking msync waiters, and WaitAny and WaitAll on local events, semaphores
and mutexes, untimed or with a zero timeout. Its client and server files are renamed to
msync.c and msync.h, with its functions, types and debug channel, as the x86_64 runtime has
them. As before:
- it is on unless WINEMSYNC=0 (WINEUSYNC and WINEWFUSYNC are read as well), and both sides
  decide the same way;
- the shared state is mapped in fixed 64K granules, which a wineserver with 16K pages and
  clients with 4K pages agree on;
- abandon_mutexes() collects the abandoned mutexes before waking their waiters.

On ARM, USync 6 masks signals while it holds its locks, rather than deferring them as on x86_64,
so signal_arm64.c is unchanged. The ARM64EC vector register context, which source/wine
reverted, stays reverted.
